# R5: exact named worker CDC port

The reviewed R2 image has actually started in SRAM. Root's kernel observation
found worker USB2E8A:000A, serial M0R55POW01, with the native macOS callout
`/dev/cu.usbmodemM0R55POW011`. The unchanged R3 numerical suffix check rejects
that path before an open. No attempt was made to open it under R3 or create an
alias. R5 adds only this exact literal path to the existing numeric admission,
and only for stage `math-cdc` with `worker_USB_serial=M0R55POW01`.

The rest of LiveLease/LeasePort is byteexact R3: canonical/grant equality,
hash/owner/UID/scope/stage/expiry checks, exact rdev before and after open,
instance lock/exclusive FD, every native operation's live guard, budgets and
owned descriptor cleanup. The worker stage and payload are unchanged. The
supervisor differs only by narrowing its CLI to math-cdc. Resident stages use
the already reviewed operators. No firmware, math, dataset or RAM load repeats.

Three inert controls reject the named path with the wrong worker serial, in a
different stage, and with a foreign suffix, all before any native stat/open.
The exact path and the original numeric route pass with only an instance-local
fake IO. No hardware is opened by these controls. This frozen envelope is not
an execution grant: Root must issue the fresh exact worker lease and argv.
